# The workspace's companion database: Microsoft SQL Server 2025, built by the
# forge from this directory and published beside the devcontainer as
# <namespace>/<repository>-db:<rev>, then deployed by the devpod root as its own
# job on the mesh. The workspace reaches it at $DB_HOST:$DB_PORT.
#
# STILL `db', NOT `mssql'. The service name is the workspace's DB_HOST/DB_PORT
# pair, its Consul service, its host volume and its bastion target. None of
# those say which engine answers, and renaming them would edit six roster files
# to express nothing.
#
# FROM THE PLATFORM'S HOSTED REPOSITORY, BY NAME. The package policy
# (nexus-uar/config/package-policy.json, block-docker-unvetted-databases)
# refuses database images from Docker Hub: "Database images must come from
# the platform team's hosted repository". That repository is
# oci-images-internal, named here as a repository and never as an address --
# a committed file must not carry a cluster IP, which is wrong on every other
# datacenter, and the pipeline's base-image step puts this datacenter's pull
# door in front of it. The image arrives there via ci/base-images.json and
# `make -C terraform/live/gitlab mirror-base-images'; until that has run on a
# datacenter, the build fails naming this reference, which is the correct
# failure.
#
# WHAT IT IS A COPY OF. github.com/infrashift/trusted-service-containers mirrors
# mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/rhel/server:2025-latest digest-for-digest, verifying
# Microsoft's Notary signature against a pinned root CA and signing identity.
# The mirror never rebuilds, so this image IS Microsoft's, byte for byte: the
# entrypoint, the uid and the data directory below are theirs, not ours. The
# RHEL 10 base is chosen over the Ubuntu mssql/server repository to match the
# platform's UBI posture.
#
# PRE-PROMOTION, DELIBERATELY. That repository's pull request #9 is open and its
# release workflow has not promoted this image to trusted/ yet -- trusted/mssql
# does not exist. The DIGEST does not change on promotion, only the path, so
# when it lands nothing here moves: ci/base-images.json re-points its upstream
# and re-mirrors, and this FROM line is already correct. Two cautions: that
# repository's cleanup workflow DELETES the development/ image when the pull
# request closes (promote first, then close), and versions.json onboards mssql
# in `observe' expecting a ~700 MB RHEL image to quarantine, in which case
# promotion lands under quarantine/ and no trusted/ path ever appears.
#
# PINNED BY DIGEST, because the build refuses a tag: a tag can be moved, and two
# builds of one revision would then build FROM different bytes while claiming
# the same input. This is a bare amd64 manifest -- Microsoft ships no index and
# supports x86-64 hosts only -- so the digest below is the manifest's own, not
# an index's. Read it with
#   skopeo inspect --raw docker://<door>/oci-images-internal/mssql@sha256:54b58c3a...
FROM oci-images-internal/mssql@sha256:54b58c3af8156b1b25049ac2c9c47ae18e422b537dbf251588b2d45278f1fd1a

# The container refuses to start without the EULA accepted and a system
# administrator password that meets SQL Server's own policy: at least 8
# characters from three of the four classes. A password that fails it does not
# produce a clear message -- sqlservr exits and the allocation restarts -- which
# is why `demopass', the trio the PostgreSQL seeds bake, could not be reused.
#
# A DEMO CREDENTIAL, baked into the image exactly as those seeds bake
# demouser/demopass, and readable by anyone who can pull this namespace. It
# gates a workspace's throwaway database and nothing else; the README says so.
# Developer edition is free for non-production use, which is what a workspace is
# -- naming a production edition here would assert a licence nobody holds.
ENV ACCEPT_EULA=Y \
    MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD=DemoPass-123 \
    MSSQL_PID=Developer

# NOT APPLIED ON FIRST START, and that is a real difference from the PostgreSQL
# seeds. SQL Server's image has no init-script hook: there is no equivalent of
# /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d, and the mirror cannot add one without ceasing to
# be a byte-identical copy. It ships here for the developer to apply once --
# services/db/init.sql says how -- and it is also what makes the image
# demonstrably non-empty: the build refuses a service image whose largest layer
# is under 512 bytes, and a Containerfile of ENV lines alone produces exactly
# that.
COPY init.sql /var/opt/mssql-init/00-workspace.sql
